  "narrative": "Wave 1 provides discovery evidence for 12 broad, cell-safe comparisons. No claim meets a sufficiently strict evidence bar for acceptance: none has a signed 95% interval whose lower bound exceeds the approximately 10% relative clinical-relevance threshold in the hypothesized direction, and none has a precise interval excluding the null while also indicating a clinically meaningful magnitude. Because these are 12 simultaneous observational screens, I applied multiplicity caution and did not treat borderline confidence-interval crossings as discoveries. For claims where the signed upper bound was clearly below a 10% relative effect in the hypothesized direction, I rejected the directional claim even though the intervals sometimes left open the possibility of an opposite association: H4, H7, H9, and H10 had maximum supported effects in the claimed direction that were too small to be clinically meaningful. I did not accept those opposite effects because reversing after a null screen would be data-driven and would worsen multiplicity.\n\nH3 is the only candidate strong enough to warrant independent validation. Its signed estimate for outcome_006 feature_060=1 versus 0 is +0.0194, corresponding to a raw negative contrast (stronger dependency in the exposed group). Relative to the inferred comparator mean for outcome_006, this point estimate is roughly in the 10-12% clinically relevant range, and the interval upper bound is well above that threshold. However, the interval also includes zero and negative signed values, so this dataset alone does not establish the effect. I therefore marked H3 unresolved but active and requested validation. If validation confirms the signed negative raw contrast with an independent interval consistent with a clinically meaningful magnitude, H3 could be upgraded; if validation attenuates or reverses, it should remain non-accepted or rejected.\n\nH1, H2, H6, and H8 are left unresolved but closed: their point estimates are very small or in the wrong direction, although their intervals are wide enough that I cannot formally exclude a clinically meaningful effect. H5, H11, and H12 are unresolved and deferred because they involve relatively common binary markers (feature_002 and feature_034) with signed point estimates around 1.4-1.6 units, below threshold but in plausible directions, and they motivate focused subgroup-context follow-ups rather than broad new screening. I registered refinements of the near-signal markers: the feature_060 effect in a feature_002-defined subgroup, feature_002 effects in feature_034 and feature_008 contexts, and the feature_034 effect in a feature_002 context. These comparisons have large cells and remain within the same discovery dataset, so they can refine where the initial weak signals reside without immediately claiming generalizability. All conclusions remain observational; predictor labels are opaque, confounding or cohort structure cannot be ruled out, and causal interpretation is not supported at this stage.",
